Declare PAN support in the board header, where every translation unit sees it.

This macro was only ever defined by Apple's own SoC headers, so on this port
user_access_enable() compiled to nothing and arm_init never turned PAN on, while
SCTLR_EL1.SPAN=0 makes the CPU set PAN itself on every exception entry: each
syscall therefore reached the copy routines with PAN set and every user store
faulted. Declaring it here also enables the PAN initialisation in arm_init, the
context-switch assertion in pcb.c and PSR64_PAN in the kernel CPSR.

--- a/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/KEBAB.h
+++ b/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/KEBAB.h
@@ -23,6 +23,14 @@
 #define __ARM_ARCH__              8
 
 /*
+ * SM8250 implements PAN (ARMv8.1+). Declaring it here - not in one .c file - is
+ * what makes arm_init turn PAN on at boot, pcb.c assert it across context
+ * switches, and proc_reg.h put PSR64_PAN in the kernel CPSR. Without that the
+ * kernel runs with PAN off and copyio's user_access_enable() is a no-op.
+ */
+#define __ARM_PAN_AVAILABLE__     1
+
